# Kerbin

Kerbin is a Gemini-to-HTTP proxy at kerbin.northwire.xyz that lets you read public HTTP and HTTPS web pages in your Gemini client, extracting readable content and converting it to Gemtext.

## Use Kerbin with Lagrange

1. Open Preferences, then Proxies (on mobile, the Network panel), and set the HTTP proxy field to the address below.

```
kerbin.northwire.xyz:1964
```

2. Save, then open any ordinary http:// or https:// URL in Lagrange. The HTTP proxy field covers both schemes; enter only the host and port, without gemini://, and include :1964 because Lagrange defaults to port 1965, which serves this guide rather than the proxy.

=> https://example.com/ Try reading a web page through Kerbin
=> gemini://kerbin.northwire.xyz/ Return to this guide

## Other Gemini clients

Your client must support HTTP and HTTPS scheme proxies over Gemini: set the proxy to kerbin.northwire.xyz on port 1964, then navigate to the original web URL without wrapping it in gemini://. A browser-style HTTP proxy or HTTP CONNECT setting will not work with this service.

## What to expect

* Web pages become text, headings, lists, quotes, and links.
* Links stay as ordinary web URLs, so your proxy setting also handles the next page.
* Image links can be opened or shown inline by clients such as Lagrange.
* Other file types keep their original type, and viewing them depends on your client.
* JavaScript, sign-ins, and form submissions are not supported.
* Pages may come from a short-lived cache.
* Only public destinations on HTTP port 80 and HTTPS port 443 are supported.

Very large or complex pages may be refused, and a temporary rate or capacity limit means you should wait before trying again.

## Known issues

Fetching https://www.fsf.org/ currently fails because its TLS cipher suites are incompatible with Kerbin's TLS backend. We are aware of the issue.
